WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.650 --> 00:00:05.650
So in the previous section,
we introduced character tables,

00:00:05.650 --> 00:00:08.240
and we saw that we can think
about a multiple alignment

00:00:08.240 --> 00:00:12.240
as a character table of its own, for
which each of the m columns of this

00:00:13.430 --> 00:00:15.680
multiple alignment can be
viewed as a character.

00:00:17.650 --> 00:00:20.100
Our goal is to use this multiple alignment

00:00:20.100 --> 00:00:22.930
to reconstruct the most
likely ancestral sequences.

00:00:24.770 --> 00:00:28.340
To do so, we're going to need
some way of determining how good

00:00:28.340 --> 00:00:31.310
an assignment of ancestral
sequences is for a given tree.

00:00:32.390 --> 00:00:35.420
For example, say that we're given
the following tree structure for

00:00:35.420 --> 00:00:39.730
the four species that we've been
considering, and that we then assign

00:00:39.730 --> 00:00:42.760
the following ancestral sequences
to internal nodes of the tree.

00:00:46.170 --> 00:00:48.250
We're going to define the parsimony score,
then,

00:00:48.250 --> 00:00:52.860
of this tree, as the sum of the Hamming
distances along each edge of the tree.

00:00:54.450 --> 00:00:56.580
This tree has parsimony
score equal to eight.

00:00:57.840 --> 00:01:02.840
And this discussion brings us to
the small parsimony problem, in which

00:01:02.840 --> 00:01:07.940
we're given a rooted binary tree whose
leaves are labeled by strings of length m,

00:01:07.940 --> 00:01:11.800
and our goal is to assign
ancestral sequences to this tree

00:01:11.800 --> 00:01:13.730
in order to minimize the parsimony score.

00:01:15.880 --> 00:01:19.873
Now, this seems like a difficult problem.

00:01:19.873 --> 00:01:21.440
Is there going to be any
way of simplifying it?

00:01:25.340 --> 00:01:29.706
If we treat the columns of the multiple
alignment that we're given as independent

00:01:29.706 --> 00:01:33.182
of each other, then it means that
we'll be able to solve the small

00:01:33.182 --> 00:01:37.129
parsimony problem on each column of
the multiple alignment separately.

00:01:38.660 --> 00:01:41.850
In other words, we can work with one
symbol of each string at a time.

00:01:44.720 --> 00:01:49.341
So we'll now design a dynamic programming 
algorithm that will solve

00:01:49.341 --> 00:01:54.260
the small parsimony problem, assuming
that each string is just a single symbol.

00:01:55.620 --> 00:02:00.122
So first, define T_v as the subtree of

00:02:00.122 --> 00:02:04.638
the given tree T whose root is node v.

00:02:04.638 --> 00:02:10.166
Then define s_k(v) as
the minimum parsimony score

00:02:10.166 --> 00:02:15.695
of the subtree T_v over all
possible labelings of T_v,

00:02:15.695 --> 00:02:20.341
such that the node v is
labeled by symbol k.

00:02:20.341 --> 00:02:24.034
Note that according to this definition,

00:02:24.034 --> 00:02:29.202
the minimum parsimony score of
the entire tree T is equal to

00:02:29.202 --> 00:02:34.701
the minimum value of s_k at the root,
taken over all symbols k.

00:02:34.701 --> 00:02:39.178
The idea of our dynamic programming
algorithm, then, is going to start at

00:02:39.178 --> 00:02:42.874
the leaves of the tree,
then compute the values s_k upward from

00:02:42.874 --> 00:02:47.870
the leaves to the root, and then use this
fact to find the minimum parsimony score.

00:02:50.140 --> 00:02:52.050
In order to implement this algorithm,

00:02:52.050 --> 00:02:55.430
we're going to need a recurrence
relation for the score of a node v in

00:02:55.430 --> 00:02:59.440
terms of its two children,
which I'll call its "son" and its "daughter".

00:03:01.230 --> 00:03:05.500
So we can define sigma_i,j
to be 0 if the symbols i and

00:03:05.500 --> 00:03:07.250
j are equal to each other.

00:03:07.250 --> 00:03:09.150
And if i and
j are not equal to each other,

00:03:09.150 --> 00:03:12.680
we define sigma_i,j to be equal to 1.

00:03:12.680 --> 00:03:16.810
Because you're a dynamic
programming expert by now,

00:03:16.810 --> 00:03:19.790
I'm going to leave proving this
recurrence relation to you.

00:03:21.990 --> 00:03:24.840
So once you've demonstrated
this recurrence relation,

00:03:24.840 --> 00:03:26.490
let's see how it works for an example.

00:03:28.180 --> 00:03:33.580
So at each node v, an array is going to
hold the set of scores s_k(v) for

00:03:33.580 --> 00:03:35.670
each possible symbol k.

00:03:35.670 --> 00:03:38.290
Here we're going to be working with DNA, so
there will be four symbols k.

00:03:40.420 --> 00:03:42.845
Because the leaves
are preassigned in our tree,

00:03:42.845 --> 00:03:47.720
we're going to set their scores equal to
0 for the symbols of those leaves, and

00:03:47.720 --> 00:03:49.750
infinity for all other symbols.

00:03:49.750 --> 00:03:52.720
For example,
here the leaf is assigned C, so

00:03:52.720 --> 00:03:55.890
we set the score of C equal to 0 and
the score of T equal to infinity.

00:03:56.970 --> 00:04:01.430
Why would we do this? Well,
it prevents us from then going back and

00:04:01.430 --> 00:04:03.320
changing the leaf node to t later.

00:04:03.320 --> 00:04:05.260
It is something that we want to prevent, right?

00:04:05.260 --> 00:04:11.378
So when we move up a level, we're going
to apply the recurrence relation.

00:04:11.378 --> 00:04:17.090
In the left most node,
the two children are fixed as equal to C.

00:04:17.090 --> 00:04:20.760
So the score of this node is
going to be 0 if we assign it a C,

00:04:20.760 --> 00:04:23.150
since there will be no conflicts
between these two edges.

00:04:24.180 --> 00:04:26.650
If we assign this node any other symbol,

00:04:26.650 --> 00:04:31.460
then there will be two conflicts between
the edges, and its score is going to be 2.

00:04:31.460 --> 00:04:37.050
One node over from this, we will obtain a
score of 1 if we assign this node an A or

00:04:37.050 --> 00:04:40.910
a C, and a score of 2 otherwise
because we'll have two conflicts.

00:04:42.190 --> 00:04:43.900
The remaining two nodes proceed similarly.

00:04:45.990 --> 00:04:51.712
So, note that I'm using red to indicate
the minimum score at each step and

00:04:51.712 --> 00:04:55.367
there may be a tie as we've
seen in two nodes here.

00:04:55.367 --> 00:04:57.626
When we move up one
more level in the tree,

00:04:57.626 --> 00:05:01.706
let's walk through the computation of,
say, the array of the left node.

00:05:04.120 --> 00:05:09.047
The score of A corresponds to
the minimum parsimony score

00:05:09.047 --> 00:05:13.665
over all possible
assignments to this subtree,

00:05:13.665 --> 00:05:17.580
assuming that A is
assigned to that subtree.

00:05:19.680 --> 00:05:24.200
In this case, the best that we can
do is not to take an A here, but

00:05:24.200 --> 00:05:28.790
to take a C here, since the parsimony
score of that subtree will be equal to 0.

00:05:28.790 --> 00:05:33.610
And then that will provide us with
a conflict of 1 on that edge.

00:05:34.630 --> 00:05:38.500
In this case, we do want to take A because
the parsimony score of this subtree will

00:05:38.500 --> 00:05:41.240
be 1, and then A will agree here.

00:05:41.240 --> 00:05:44.970
In a sense, we're breaking the tie between
A and C if we assign that equal to A.

00:05:46.050 --> 00:05:50.150
So we would have gotten a score
of 1 from this subtree, and

00:05:50.150 --> 00:05:53.620
a score of 1 from this subtree,
so we assign A a score of 2.

00:05:53.620 --> 00:05:59.800
C should be pretty straightforward because
C is a minimum at each of these two nodes.

00:05:59.800 --> 00:06:04.720
So if we assign a C here and a C here,
we get parsimoniy scores of 0 and 1,

00:06:04.720 --> 00:06:06.810
and we have no conflicts here.

00:06:06.810 --> 00:06:09.580
So this is really what
the recurrence relation is saying.

00:06:09.580 --> 00:06:11.405
So, two more examples.

00:06:11.405 --> 00:06:16.773
For G, if we assume that we assign a G
there, then we don't want to assign

00:06:16.773 --> 00:06:22.147
Gs here because that would give us
parsimony score of 4 here and 4 here,

00:06:22.147 --> 00:06:26.536
and although we wouldn't have
any conflicts along these edges,

00:06:26.536 --> 00:06:28.381
we would have a score of 4.

00:06:28.381 --> 00:06:33.902
We can do better than that if we simply
assign Cs to both of these nodes,

00:06:33.902 --> 00:06:39.341
in which case we get two conflicting
edges, so a contribution of 2,

00:06:39.341 --> 00:06:42.394
but then a contribution of 0 and
1, which gives us 3.

00:06:42.394 --> 00:06:48.037
The case of T follows analogously
because it's essentially the same as G.

00:06:48.037 --> 00:06:53.655
So hopefully, you can see from that how
we can get the right node on this level,

00:06:53.655 --> 00:06:56.430
as well as when we move to the root.

00:06:56.430 --> 00:06:57.469
So when we move to the root,

00:06:58.740 --> 00:07:02.830
the minimum parsimony score is going
to be corresponding to symbol C,

00:07:02.830 --> 00:07:06.830
which means that we should assign C to
the root because we said that the minimum

00:07:06.830 --> 00:07:10.970
parsimony score of the tree was
the minimum at the root over all symbols.

00:07:12.510 --> 00:07:16.800
So, to reconstruct the other ancestral
states, you're going to need to flex your

00:07:16.800 --> 00:07:22.100
dynamic programming muscles once again and
implement a backtracking approach

00:07:22.100 --> 00:07:26.700
that goes from the root down to
the leaves and assign symbols.

00:07:26.700 --> 00:07:31.690
I want you to note, when you do this, that
the red elements correspond to minima, but

00:07:31.690 --> 00:07:35.820
they don't necessarily correspond to an
optimal assignment of ancestral symbols.

00:07:35.820 --> 00:07:38.490
So, for example,
we're going to get into trouble

00:07:38.490 --> 00:07:42.380
if we assign a T to this
right-most node in the tree.

00:07:43.750 --> 00:07:46.420
So, I want you to make sure that your
backtracking approach is able to

00:07:46.420 --> 00:07:51.860
reconstruct the tree shown on this slide
before you try to implement a solution for

00:07:51.860 --> 00:07:53.050
the small parsimony problem.

00:07:56.030 --> 00:07:57.820
Once you have done so,

00:07:57.820 --> 00:08:00.730
you might find it interesting to
apply the SmallParsimony dynamic

00:08:00.730 --> 00:08:04.945
programming algorithm to the tree we
previously constructed for coronaviruses.

00:08:06.120 --> 00:08:09.040
What are the ancestral coronavirus
sequences that you obtain?

00:08:10.700 --> 00:08:15.170
Also, it turns out that you can find the
most parsimonious labeling of the internal

00:08:15.170 --> 00:08:20.430
nodes in an unrooted tree by placing
a root on an arbitrary edge,

00:08:20.430 --> 00:08:24.540
solving the small parsimony problem for
the resulting rooted tree, and

00:08:24.540 --> 00:08:26.200
then simply removing the root.

